// Practice quiz: Schema migrations with Flyway
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/schema-migrations-with-flyway

::o1 naming::Which file name is a valid Flyway versioned migration? {
~V3_add_price.sql#Not quite. That has one underscore; two are required.
~3__add_price.sql#Not quite. It is missing the V prefix.
~add_price_V3.sql#Not quite. The version must come first.
=V3__add_price.sql#Correct. V, a version, two underscores and a description.
}

::o1 location::Where does Boot look for migrations by default? {
=classpath\:db/migration#Correct. That is src/main/resources/db/migration.
~src/main/java#Not quite. That holds Java sources.
~target/flyway#Not quite. That is build output.
~The project root#Not quite. The default is the classpath location.
}

::o2 applied migration::What does Flyway do with an already applied, unchanged migration on startup? {
~Runs it again#Not quite. Applied migrations do not re-run.
~Deletes it#Not quite. Flyway does not delete files.
~Fails startup#Not quite. Unchanged files validate normally.
=Skips it#Correct. It only validates the recorded checksum.
}

::o2 edited migration::You edit an already applied migration file. What happens on the next start? {
~The new statements are applied#Not quite. Applied versions are not re-run.
~The change is silently ignored#Not quite. Validation catches it.
=Validation fails with a checksum mismatch#Correct. The stored checksum no longer matches.
~A new version number is assigned#Not quite. Flyway does not renumber files.
}

::o3 ddl-auto::Once Flyway owns the schema, which ddl-auto setting fits? {
~create-drop#Not quite. That destroys the schema.
=validate or none#Correct. Hibernate must not change the schema.
~update#Not quite. That can change the schema outside migrations.
~create#Not quite. That recreates the schema.
}

